`canUpdateVitestConfigFile` returns `true` for `mergeConfig` patterns where the config object inside the call is not actually accessible. For example, `export default mergeConfig(viteConfig, 'string')` or `export default mergeConfig(viteConfig, someOpaqueVariable)` will cause `getEffectiveMergeConfigCall` to return a non-null value (the mergeConfig call is found), but none of `mergeConfig`'s arguments yield an `ObjectExpression` via `getConfigObjectFromMergeArg`. The function then reports the file as updatable, but `updateVitestFile` will silently fail (returning `false`) because `configObjectNodes` will be empty at line 465 of `updateVitestFile.ts`.

The old `isValidVitestConfig` correctly handled this: `isSafeToExtendWorkspace` returned `false` for non-object/non-call arguments, so `mergeCall.arguments.some(arg => isSafeToExtendWorkspace(arg))` returned `false` for those inputs.

To fix, when `getEffectiveMergeConfigCall` is non-null, additionally verify that at least one argument yields a valid config object:
```ts
const mergeCall = getEffectiveMergeConfigCall(exportDefault.declaration, parsedAst);
if (mergeCall !== null) {
  return mergeCall.arguments.some(
    (arg) => getConfigObjectFromMergeArg(arg as t.Expression, parsedAst) !== null
  );
}
return false;
```
